WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.589
It's great to be joined today by Tara Isabella
Burton, who is the author of Strange Rights

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:10.290
New Religions for a Godless World and the
Novel Social Creature.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.900
Tara, it's so great to have you on.
Oh, thank you so much for having me. I'm delighted

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.019
to be here.
So many of us and many in my audience have

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:23.689
seen the studies that show that adherence
to organized religion and identification as

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:30.160
as religious and as members of individual
religions have declined in the United States.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.640
But there is another sort of side to this,
which is the idea of spirituality, which can

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.550
take a lot of different forms. And you talk
about ways in which while sort of more traditional

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.140
religiosity is being rejected, that we're
not necessarily seeing a decrease in this

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.750
broader umbrella of spirituality. Can you
talk about how spirituality is different than

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:56.000
religiosity and maybe give us some examples
of where we're seeing it? Sure.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.629
So just for a little bit of background, like
as you say, about a quarter of Americans identify

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:08.150
as religiously unaffiliated. Often they call
the Enno any nones. That number goes up to

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:14.230
36 percent of Americans born after 1985. So
we're talking about a huge proportion of particularly

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:19.441
millennial and younger population. However,
that doesn't mean, as you say, that they're

00:01:19.441 --> 00:01:25.540
not interested in spirituality and spirituality,
at least as as I understand it. And as I work

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.540
on it and my book is about sort of various
elements of religious life, unbundled, you

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:38.150
might say, divorced from organized religion,
which which is itself about so much more than

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:44.060
just personal belief, organized religion,
and indeed any religion, you might say, has

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.810
sort of a component that is belief and dogma,
a search for meaning, individual purpose,

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:55.650
but also community understanding of a kind
of social place and the rituals that kind

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:01.430
of bind that community together. And what
we're seeing isn't necessarily is that spirituality

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:06.090
is somehow different from organized religion
and offering those different things. But often

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:12.360
that we find people, young Americans, particular,
more willing to find these different components

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:18.540
of religious life from a variety of sources.
Someone might, for example, still go to like

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.231
Christmas and Easter services or various high
holy days with their families a couple of

00:02:23.231 --> 00:02:29.030
times a year, have a sense of community from
a hobby that they do also practice meditation

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:37.980
or yoga kind of privately and really just
creating their own religion rather than sort

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.460
of adopting wholesale the model of an existing
organized tradition.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.240
So what's the difference between some of what
you describe and just a lifestyle in the sense

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:54.830
that I mean, one could imagine that one finds,
for example, veganism, yoga and bicycling

00:02:54.830 --> 00:03:02.290
to be sort of related to the values that they
exhibit and dealing with the problems they

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.900
most acutely identify as needing solutions
or whatever. How do we how would we distinguish

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:13.319
that from whether it is a lifestyle, whether
it has a sort of spiritualistic component,

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:17.260
whether it is more religious? What are the
lines? Or maybe it's a gray area.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.830
So I actually think it is a gray area. I think
it's almost impossible to actually have a

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:29.800
working definition of religion. You see this
actually in other areas where people are often

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.480
sort of talking about cults. What makes a
cult versus a quote unquote, normal religion?

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.280
The lines are blurry. Sometimes I joke. It's
like porn. You know what, when you see it.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.830
But the same time, even within sort of academic
definitions of religion, like there are as

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.730
many definitions of religion as there are
scholars of religion. In my book, I talk about

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.530
sort of Durkheim or Clifford Giertz, all of
whom see religion as completely different.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:59.240
It's about belief. No, it's about society.
No, it's about symbols and sort of a shared

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.830
understanding. All of these things are also
true more broadly. So I think a useful way

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:09.580
to think about it is not kind of is this a
religion, is it not a religion? But looking

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:14.959
at the ways in which organized religions have
traditionally been kind of transmission vectors

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:21.540
or places for a lot of the components of religious
life all at once, you know, your your sense

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.880
of meaning, your sense of purpose, your sense
of community and your sense of ritual largely

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.930
coming from one place. This was all the more
true when, for example, where you worshiped

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.990
on Sunday or any other day of the week, for
that matter, would also be where you met people.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.830
You were most likely to perhaps form a relationship
and marry and have children with a community

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:46.620
that sort of supported you through your various
life stages, your values, your sense of ritual,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:52.720
your sense of meaning and purpose were tied
up. And I say your as or traditionally in

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.680
America more broadly like. Sort of this the
stereotype of the 1950s where what church

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:05.100
you went to was a sort of byword for a whole
other host of social associations. And that's

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.870
really what we've lost in the quote unquote,
spiritual, but not religious or secular age

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.360
rather than belief or interest in the metaphysical
or the divine.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.150
Well, thinking earlier than the 1950s, in
earlier human history, there was a time which

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:23.760
I find very interesting, where a membership
in a religious group actually conferred an

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:30.320
evolutionary advantage in the sense that you
were far more likely if you got along with

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.610
the people around you than if you didn't to
survive and to have enough food to eat and

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:41.271
to go on to have children and procreate and
pass on your genes. That element of religion

00:05:41.271 --> 00:05:45.510
has dramatically diminished. Certainly, we
could probably find some places in the world

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:50.090
where it exists, but but in modern Western
world that that's not a factor. I'm interested

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:59.650
in whether you you see this sort of modern
lifestyle slash spirituality vector's having

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:06.729
some component of that advantage in society,
maybe not literally to our survival, but to

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:12.449
some degree of prospering maybe. Yes.
I mean, yes and no. And what I mean by that

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:17.270
is simultaneously, I think that the kind of
new tribalism that we've formed through various

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:24.290
affiliations, often by as distinct from religions
and to which we were often born, are determined

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:29.900
by choice. You know, we are part of this group
or that group. Our interest in cross fit to

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:36.979
toe or what have you. Gives us a sort of family
we choose in a very particular way. At the

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:41.390
same time, though, I think that so much of
the way in which the sort of contemporary

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:49.410
spirituality works is through a quite individualized
model. We basically each make our own religion

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:55.250
in that way. And I say we sort of more broadly,
but. Particularly, young Americans tend to

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.450
be more likely to mix and match even. And
this isn't exclusive to people who call themselves

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:08.100
religious nuns. There was a 2013 Pew study
that found that about. 30 percent of self-identified

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.590
Christians also said, like, I believe in reincarnations.
About a similar number said they believe in

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:18.169
psychics, astrology, things that are not traditionally
understood within the Orthodox Christian umbrella.

00:07:18.169 --> 00:07:21.960
We're seeing more broadly a willingness on
the part of both the religious, traditionally

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:28.880
religious and the unaffiliated to kind of
create through through choice, through what

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.570
they buy, the classes they take. It's often
a very sort consumerist choice, their own

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:40.400
sort of spiritual identity, especially any
Internet based capitalist world such as we

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:48.240
are in. That often means voting with your
dollars kind of consuming as a form of creating.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.550
Do you buy the Sephora, which kit, which has
its starter pack of tarot cards, which was

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:58.690
for a while available on kind of the high
street until it was actually withdrawn over

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:04.500
a bit of controversy? Do you spend your money
on a yoga class or meditation app? And I think

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.560
the way in which we've become sort of more
atomized, more individual in part because

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:18.110
of this sort of obsession with our own choices,
our own affinities, has made perhaps this

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:23.340
iteration of spirituality a little bit less
group focused. I would say, than religion

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.780
is traditionally understood.
Well, one of the interesting things that you

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:32.459
mentioned gets into some of the examples that
relate to one of the characteristics that's

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:40.599
often present in certainly the more devout
areas of the traditional religions is that

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:46.649
there is not a very strong sort of error correcting
mechanism the way science has where questioning

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:51.839
the beliefs and changing the beliefs is is
welcomed. A lot of times the community leader

00:08:51.839 --> 00:08:57.579
in more established religious religions doesn't
welcome people coming in and saying, I think

00:08:57.579 --> 00:09:00.550
that you're actually wrong about this, that
the other thing and saying, hey, we made a

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:06.029
mistake, let's change what the what the scriptures
are. That's often not encouraged. And so when

00:09:06.029 --> 00:09:12.350
I look at some of the modern ways in which
we might explore spirituality or religion,

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.910
I sort of pay attention to that. And so, for
example, I had an experience with the followers

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:22.329
of this gentleman named Jordan Peterson a
couple of years ago, where there were a lot

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:26.790
of examples of when I would critique something
he said or did. There was a sort of post hoc

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:32.629
rationalization of your misunderstand you.
You have to be misunderstanding what he said

00:09:32.629 --> 00:09:39.370
and therefore your critique is not accurate.
Or another example might be either flat earth

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:45.670
or astrology, where you might try to say,
hey, let's try to apply some critical thinking.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:53.509
And the idea of revising the dogma as it may
be, is not at all welcomed. Is that the case

00:09:53.509 --> 00:09:59.110
in a lot of these spirituality outlets that
you look at? Not in a lot.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:04.399
How present is that? It's present. But I'd
say a slightly different form, which is to

00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:11.690
say often these groups, or rather that these
practices as instantiated in individuals have

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:16.819
come up in part because they're sort of we
have such a profound public distrust now in

00:10:16.819 --> 00:10:21.720
institutions. There is a sort of, you know,
particularly religious institutions from sex

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:28.569
abuse scandals to the political alliance of
particularly white evangelical Christianity,

00:10:28.569 --> 00:10:33.300
with the GOP to say nothing of sort of the
broader failures of institutions we see, whether

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.860
it's sort of the sense of the media or politics
or what have you. There is, I would argue,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.860
a widespread sense that because institutions
have failed, we can no longer trust them.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:45.920
What do you do when you can't trust the institution,
when you can't trust science, or you think

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.050
you can't trust science or journalists or
the establishment? You turn it inwards. There's

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:57.050
a sense in which it is sort of similarly,
perhaps it is. I do want to say anti intellectual.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:01.480
Perhaps that's too strong, but I'm intuitional.
There are a lot of these movements from wellness

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:06.980
culture to to an extent, even some of the
followers of Jordan, Peter said in a certain

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.820
way, although he is the sort of leader which
adds a different dimension. But the idea that

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:15.259
you focus, you turn inward, you further focus
on yourself, which is what you can control

00:11:15.259 --> 00:11:20.899
and what you can rely on. And I think that
that you often find that in a lot of the conspiracy

00:11:20.899 --> 00:11:25.279
theorists and even the way that a lot of like
Jordan Peterson fans will sort of talk about

00:11:25.279 --> 00:11:31.970
him versus the sort of the P.C. establishment
that's hiding troops from you. There is a

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:39.100
very much an anti-establishment A.I. institutional
strain to a lot of these beliefs. And so I

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:44.550
think on the one hand, there is a rejection
of kind of dogma in the abstract as something

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:48.629
that institutions do that we don't want to
sort of succumb to. And yet there is also

00:11:48.629 --> 00:11:53.839
a resistance to, for example, in the case
of like wellness culture, you know, very few

00:11:53.839 --> 00:12:00.839
of the scientific facts on Gwyneth Paltrow
website Goop are we might say they are not

00:12:00.839 --> 00:12:04.660
always fact checked to the fullest extent.
And indeed, they, I think, gotten quite a

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:09.110
bit of trouble over that. But I think the
narrative goes something like, well, like

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:15.269
this feels true and it's completely understandable
that when a person might think that none of

00:12:15.269 --> 00:12:21.720
the authorities around them are worth listening
to, they can't rely on them suddenly to feel

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.809
like going with your gut. There are worse
things you can do.

00:12:25.809 --> 00:12:30.069
I'm interested in talking a little bit about
the protests we've seen over the last couple

00:12:30.069 --> 00:12:37.149
of weeks, protests that I widely support the
objectives of and in which I'm sympathetic

00:12:37.149 --> 00:12:42.639
to. I've seen some examples. I don't have,
you know, feel the need to say this is a lot

00:12:42.639 --> 00:12:47.730
or not a lot. But I've seen examples of would
seem almost like pseudo religious types of

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:53.579
moments where black people in large groups
are I'm sorry, white people in large groups

00:12:53.579 --> 00:13:00.319
apologize to black people for their privilege
or calls for white people to give the black

00:13:00.319 --> 00:13:05.309
people at the protests money in some kind
of symbolic reparations that things that things

00:13:05.309 --> 00:13:11.139
that to me start to feel a little bit cult
and that they go beyond sort of the activism

00:13:11.139 --> 00:13:17.269
component to it. How should we be applying
this religiosity, the spirituality analysis

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:21.249
to the movement we're seeing take hold over
the last couple of weeks?

00:13:21.249 --> 00:13:24.019
Sure. I think speaking more broadly about
the social justice movement, as I do in the

00:13:24.019 --> 00:13:28.420
book, I mean, I think it's absolutely right
to apply this scholar of religions lens or

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:34.360
the religious lens to it. And I think that
when people often do that, sort of it's often

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:39.910
a charge that was leveled against the social
justice movement. The right. You know, they're

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:45.420
zealots. They're a religion. They're a cult,
et cetera. I'd like to sort of brought say

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:51.220
that do something a bit more complicated,
which is that, yes, it is religious in nature.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:14:00.019
That is both. As with any any movement, the
good and the bad it which is to say, I think

00:14:00.019 --> 00:14:04.130
the sense of hope, the sense of profundity,
the sense that this is meaningful, that there

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:10.809
is a a better world at the end of all of this,
which is, you know, quite striking, I think

00:14:10.809 --> 00:14:19.379
in our somewhat somewhat alienated capitalist
world. One might say there aren't that many

00:14:19.379 --> 00:14:25.290
places for not just the sacred or enchantment,
but a vision of that enchantment that is linked

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:29.529
up with a vision of the world hard's that
should be. Things like wellness culture are

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:33.820
very individualistic. As I've said, you know,
you great. You can you can achieve a sort

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:38.129
of enlightenment soul cycle, but it begins
and ends with you. And I think that it's absolutely

00:14:38.129 --> 00:14:43.809
right to talk about the kind of the symbolism,
the sense of catharsis, the collective effervescence

00:14:43.809 --> 00:14:48.199
as a Durkheim might say that you might find
at a protest movement as being religious in

00:14:48.199 --> 00:14:52.160
nature. But I think that's why it's that's
what I would say. That's why it's been so

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:57.269
successful, so widespread precisely because
it is able to harness a kind of religious

00:14:57.269 --> 00:15:05.519
longing. This is not just a group of people
protesting. It is people sort of getting in

00:15:05.519 --> 00:15:11.589
touch with perhaps not a divine purpose, but
a sort of fundamentally real purpose to change

00:15:11.589 --> 00:15:16.609
the world, to make it better or certainly
a purpose that's bigger than them. Yes, absolutely.

00:15:16.609 --> 00:15:20.949
Absolutely. And I think it brings it both
brings people together, which especially after

00:15:20.949 --> 00:15:27.439
a however many months of isolation, I think
is all the more appealing. And it provides

00:15:27.439 --> 00:15:34.319
a framework to kind of see an. A world that
could very well see meaningless and chaotic,

00:15:34.319 --> 00:15:39.949
some form not just of meaning in the abstract,
but but purpose. What one person's individual

00:15:39.949 --> 00:15:45.550
life, individual choices to. To protest, to
get involved. How that might link up with

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:51.709
a kind of grander cosmic bigger than them
scale. So I think it's absolutely right to

00:15:51.709 --> 00:15:56.489
think about it in terms of religion, but also
sort of the question to ask. It's not like

00:15:56.489 --> 00:16:00.910
are they a religion? And therefore, should
it be sort of discounted as a rational but

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:10.399
rather as a religion? What is this offering
that other forms of living in 2020 aren't

00:16:10.399 --> 00:16:16.369
necessarily offering? No. How what why is
this so meaningful and compelling?

00:16:16.369 --> 00:16:20.910
Very, very interesting.
We've been speaking with Tara, Izabella Burton,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:26.089
who's author of Strange writes New Religions
for a Godless World and also author of the

00:16:26.089 --> 00:16:29.800
novel Social Creature.
Tara, so great having you on. I really appreciate

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.499
it. Thank you so much. It was lovely.

